## What do insurance agency CRMs cost?

Published entry prices range from $25 monthly to $149 monthly, but those figures use different billing units.

Insureio starts at $25 monthly but does not define whether that price covers a user or account. MedicarePRO CRM starts at $45 monthly for one staff member.

NextAgency charges $85 per seat monthly. A six-person agency therefore pays $510 monthly before adding its $120 monthly commission module.

Agent CRM charges $97 monthly per account and permits extra team members without seat charges. Calling and texting carrier fees remain additional.

AgencyZoom starts at $149 monthly and includes seven users. This can cost less than a cheaper-looking per-seat plan for a growing team.

InsuredMine, GloveBoxCRM and AgentCubed publish no usable starting price. AgencyIQ quotes about $80 per seat but omits the billing frequency.

## Do you need a CRM or an agency management system?

Choose a CRM for sales relationships and an agency management system for policy operations.

An agency management system stores policies and supports billing, commissions, ACORD forms, carrier downloads and compliance. Applied Epic, AMS360, HawkSoft, EZLynx and Momentum fit this category.

A CRM manages leads, pipelines, follow-up, onboarding and retention. Most independent agencies eventually use both systems.

Several vendors blur the distinction. A buyer can therefore purchase sales automation while expecting policy administration, or purchase policy software while expecting a modern pipeline.

Ask one direct question during the demo: "Is this product the policy system of record?" The answer determines whether it can replace or only complement your current system.

## Which CRM connects with your agency management system?

InsuredMine offers the broadest named compatibility, while AgencyZoom and GloveBoxCRM each support six systems.

InsuredMine names nine integrations: AMS360, Applied Epic, HawkSoft, QQ Catalyst, Momentum, Sagitta, BenefitPoint, Dyad Nexsure and Evo.

AgencyZoom connects with six management systems. GloveBoxCRM also names six.

Agent CRM, AgencyIQ, MedicarePRO CRM and AgentCubed name no agency management system integration. That absence has different implications for each product.

AgencyIQ targets captive agents whose carriers already manage policies. MedicarePRO CRM instead connects with Medicare enrollment systems, including SunFire and SimplyEnroll.

NextAgency connects with Employee Navigator, Ease and BerniePortal. These benefits administration integrations fit group benefits agencies rather than personal-lines P&C shops.

Request the integration by exact product name before signing. "AMS integration" is not enough because support varies by management system and data direction.

## Which insurance lines does each CRM support?

The correct CRM depends on your insurance line before it depends on features or price.

AgencyZoom focuses on property and casualty. NextAgency serves life, health, Medicare, long-term care, disability and group benefits.

MedicarePRO CRM is Medicare-only. Insureio focuses on life, annuities, final expense, long-term care and disability.

AgencyIQ targets captive agents. Agent CRM and AgentCubed advertise broader multi-line coverage.

InsuredMine and GloveBoxCRM do not clearly name supported lines on their product websites. Agencies should verify line-specific workflows during the demo.

## What competing insurance CRM lists get wrong

Many competing lists combine discontinued brands, renamed products, agency management systems and generic sales platforms.

[Radiusbob no longer operates as a current standalone product](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20230201005324/en/AgencyBloc-Acquires-Radius-to-Provide-Enhanced-Capabilities-for-Benefits-Brokers). Better Agency became GloveBoxCRM after its December 2024 acquisition, so listing both creates a duplicate.

Insureio also appears in Medicare CRM roundups, although its website names life and annuity products rather than Medicare support.

Generic CRMs can store insurance data in custom fields. That does not create policy administration, carrier downloads, ACORD workflows or insurance-specific compliance controls.

## How we ranked the products

We ranked products by insurance fit, integration depth, price transparency, review evidence, trial terms and visible product activity.

Insurance specialization received priority over general feature volume. A narrow product ranked well when its workflows matched a clearly defined buyer.

Published prices were compared using their stated unit. We did not treat per-seat and per-account prices as equivalent.

G2 and Capterra scores were reported with review counts. A perfect score from one review was not treated like a lower score from hundreds.

Contradictions were preserved. Missing prices, unclear units and conflicting trial terms were not filled with estimates.

## Which products were excluded?

We excluded products that were discontinued, duplicated under a new name, primarily agency management systems or generic CRMs without insurance-native workflows.

Radiusbob was excluded because it is no longer available as a current standalone product. Better Agency was not listed separately because it became GloveBoxCRM.

Applied Epic, AMS360, HawkSoft, EZLynx and Momentum were excluded because they are agency management systems. This article ranks CRM products.

Salesforce, HubSpot, Zoho, Pipedrive and Less Annoying CRM were excluded because they are general-purpose CRMs. Insurance companies can configure them, but they are not insurance-native products.
